            "<strong>Transfer</strong> within the hospital or to another facility is a handover with movement: the receiving ward is told in advance by telephone; the patient is told and agrees; the folder, charts, drug chart, X-rays, results, property list and medicines travel with the patient; an escort appropriate to the condition goes with them — a nurse for the unstable, with oxygen, suction and the emergency drugs for the long corridor and the ambulance; vital signs are taken before leaving and on arrival; and the receiving nurse signs for the patient, the notes and the property. The relative is told where their patient has gone. For an inter-facility referral a <strong>referral letter</strong> states the diagnosis, treatment given with times and doses, the reason for referral, the patient's current state and the name and telephone number of the referring clinician, and the ambulance is booked through the National Ambulance Service on 112 or the facility's own arrangement.",
            "<strong>Discharge planning</strong> begins on admission — who will care for the patient at home, what they will need, how far they live from follow-up — and ends with a discharge that the patient can carry out. The doctor's order to discharge is confirmed; the <strong>discharge summary</strong> is written; the medicines to take home are explained one by one with <strong>teach-back</strong> — name, purpose, dose, times, how long, side effects to report, and what to do about the ones already at home; wound care, diet, activity, warning signs and whom to call for each are written down in words the patient or their carer can read, or drawn; <strong>follow-up appointments</strong>, the NHIS paperwork and any referrals to the community health nurse, the CHPS compound, physiotherapy or the social welfare officer are made; property is returned and signed for; the cannula is removed and the site checked; and the patient leaves escorted, with transport arranged, and not in a trotro with a drain in place. A patient who leaves <strong>against medical advice</strong> is assessed for capacity, told the risks in plain words, invited to return at any time, asked to sign the form — and if they refuse to sign, two staff witness the refusal — and the doctor and the family are informed; the nurse's duty of care does not end in anger. Death, absconding and transfer to the mortuary have their own procedures, covered with loss and dying in the final module.",
