            "Florence Nightingale's first lesson — that the environment heals or harms — is tested every day in a tropical ward. <strong>Ventilation</strong> dilutes airborne organisms and the heat: cross-ventilation through open windows on opposite walls, fans that move air without blowing across an open wound or dressing trolley, and in tuberculosis and measles isolation, air that flows from the corridor into the room and out through a window, never the reverse. <strong>Temperature</strong> matters at both ends — the newborn, the old and the shocked lose heat even at 28 °C under a fan, and the febrile patient suffers in a closed room at 34 °C. <strong>Light</strong> — daylight for examination of colour and jaundice, dim light at night for sleep, a working light at every bed for the night nurse. <strong>Noise</strong> is a treatment failure: a sleepless patient heals slowly, and the trolley wheels, the slammed door, the staff conversation at the station and the phone on speaker are all within the nurse's control. <strong>Privacy</strong> is a screen pulled before any care that exposes the body, a conversation held at the bedside in a lowered voice or taken elsewhere, and a toilet door that closes. <strong>Cleanliness</strong> is the daily damp-dusting of surfaces (dry dusting throws organisms into the air), the mopping of floors from the clean to the dirty end, the clean toilet and sluice, and the absence of food left at the bedside overnight. <strong>Bed spacing</strong> of at least one metre — ideally more — between beds limits droplet spread and allows a resuscitation trolley to reach every patient; a ward that admits onto mattresses on the floor must still keep that path.",
