            "Personal hygiene — the care of skin, hair, nails, mouth, eyes, ears, nose, perineum and feet — maintains the skin as the body's first barrier against infection, removes sweat, dead cells and organisms, stimulates circulation, prevents odour and the pressure, moisture and friction damage that begin in the folds of a sweating bed-bound body, and restores the sense of being a person rather than a case. It is also the longest period of unhurried contact a nurse has with a patient, and so it is the best time to <strong>assess</strong> — the skin over every pressure area, the oedema, the wound under the gown, the breathlessness on turning, the pain on moving a limb, the bruise nobody mentioned, the mood, the relative's worries — and to <strong>teach</strong>, to <strong>listen</strong> and to <strong>build trust</strong>. A ward on which bed baths are delegated entirely to the most junior person and done in silence at 05:00 has given away its best assessment tool.",
            "Hygiene practices are shaped by <strong>culture and religion</strong> (ablutions before prayer, the washing of the body by particular family members, modesty rules about who may see or touch), by <strong>age</strong> (the infant's skin that loses heat and water, the adolescent's self-consciousness, the older person's thin dry skin that needs less soap and more moisturiser), by <strong>health</strong> (the breathless patient who cannot tolerate a full wash, the person with arthritis who cannot reach her feet), by <strong>socio-economic circumstances</strong> (water carried from a standpipe, no toothbrush) and by <strong>personal preference</strong> — bathing twice a day in Ghana is usual, and the patient who has not had a bath for three days feels dirty in a way that affects recovery. The nurse <strong>assesses the level of help needed</strong> — independent, needs set-up, needs partial help, fully dependent — and gives no more help than necessary, because doing for a patient what she can do for herself takes away function; she offers choice about timing, water temperature, products and who helps; and she maintains <strong>privacy and dignity</strong> throughout: screens, a closed door, only the part being washed uncovered, a same-sex nurse where the patient prefers and the ward can provide it, and a running commentary that asks permission before each step.",
